WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:01.940
Don't vlog while biking.

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:02.960
We got canals too.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.920
So Hank, the great nation of Canada recently decided to discontinue it's penny.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.580
because it doesn't facilitate commerce and also it costs 1.5 Canadian cents to make a penny.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:17.300
But Hank, here in America we're keeping our pennies and that makes sense because our pennies also don't facilitate commerce and they cost 2.1 cents to make!

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:18.620
God I hate pennies!

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.220
Now Hank, I'll admit that the pennies are a relatively minor inefficiency,

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:28.900
but today I want to talk about three much more dramatic and important examples of how our terrible political discourse is leading to awful policy making.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:30.820
But first I'm going to ride over to the park

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.860
Okay so, first the Federal Aviation Administration,

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:37.180
which is responsible for air traffic control, and safety inspections and that kinda thing.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.200
The FAA is mostly funded by at tax on airplane tickets,

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:44.820
which like most frequent fliers, I am happy to pay because my favourite kind of air traffic is controlled air traffic,

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:47.860
and my favourite kind of landing is not crash.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:51.620
But the last five years have seen 23 short-term extensions of this tax,

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.460
mostly because Congress has been fighting about unions,

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.000
because fighting over unions helps them raise money.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:05.460
Each of these short-term extensions hurts the economy, because if you're building, say, a new air traffic control tower, it's much cheaper  to do it with a long-term contract than with a gajillion short-term contracts.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.360
Same reason your phone is cheaper if you sign a two-year contract vs a one-year contract.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.800
Right, so that's a terribly inefficient way of doing business, but it's not the crazy thing.

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.640
The crazy thing is that, over the summer, Congress completely let the FAA's funding lapse.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.560
So they couldn't collect that tax on every ticket.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:25.260
Now you're probably thinking, "this wasn't so bad! After all, the money just went into the hands of the people who were buying the tickets!"

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:31.760
No, it didn't! It went into the hands of the airline because they raised their prices,  the exact amount of the tax.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.340
I am a giant squid of anger!

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:35.620
Oh look, it's a plane.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:41.360
The only reason you're safe is because of the FAA, it needs our funding!

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.220
Anyway, Congress finally passed a long-term budget in February,

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.140
but by then, this whole brouhaha probably cost us a billion dollars.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:49.180
Example two: the highway bill.

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:53.480
So we've been doing the same thing, of these hugely inefficient short-term extensions, when it comes to our highway funding.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.260
But interestingly, in this case, no one's fighting about unions.

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:01.160
So even though everyone agrees that long-term funding deals are cheaper and  more efficient,

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:05.140
we can't pass any long-term funding deals because they involve big numbers.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:08.060
Many Republicans backed by the Tea Party don't want to vote for any long-term bill,

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:13.460
because it would involve a big number and they are opposed to big government spending.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:18.140
And so there's a huge political liability in voting for, say, a 250 billion dollar highway bill,

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:21.880
so instead, we're going to vote for ten 30 billion dollar highway bills.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:26.580
Right so, at this point a lot of conservative Nerdfighters probably think that I'm like this big pinko Communist; I'm not.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:33.160
Issue three: Medicare, a fantastic and  very popular government program in which we give medics air.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:34.720
No, wait, we give healthcare to old people.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.940
So everyone agrees that Medicare is totally unsustainable.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:41.840
By 2020, Medicare could account for 5% of our gross domestic product, which is just insane.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.960
And Republicans have proposed some interesting reform ideas for Medicare,

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:50.020
but Democrats refuse to entertain them, because of course, that would mean hurting our seniors by cutting Medicare,

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.560
and those very same seniors tend to vote for Democrats.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.840
And  it makes sense, because we all know  that spending more money to take care of our seniors leads to healthier, happier seniors.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:01.260
And we all want that, because when they're not happy they're very cranky.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.440
So it makes sense not to reform Medicare,

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:10.440
except it doesn't because, insanely, the states that spend the least per Medicare patient get the best healthcare outcomes.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:15.080
This is largely for boring reasons like preventative medicine and washing your hands and not leaving scalpels inside of people,

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:15.620
but it works.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.660
So we could totally use this data to reform Medicare and control costs,

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:19.560
but instead we won't.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.640
We'll just keep disagreeing with each other for the sake of disagreeing,

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.360
because yelling at each other is worth more political points than sensible compromise.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:29.560
And our government will continue to inhibit economic growth while also minting pennies!

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:30.560
God, I hate pennies.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.720
Alright, I try to end videos on a happy note, so here's a turtle. I'll see you on Friday.

